<!--
    kuna `option dedupitetail` (default `latch`), P8 structuring: a loop latch
    the compiler copied into each path through the body is joined back into one
    latch (p8_structure/kuna_latchjoin.rs), so the loop prints once with an `if`
    in it instead of as two loops, one directly inside the other.

    gcc 11 -O2 (-fno-pic -fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none -fno-inline),
    linked with -Ttext 0x100000:
        struct node { struct node *next; short cls; };
        void hit(struct node *p); int weigh(struct node *p);
        int ready(void); void send(int v);
        void visit(struct node *p, short cls)
        { while (p) { if (p->cls == cls) hit(p); p = p->next; } }
        int count(struct node *p, short cls)
        { int n = 0;
          while (p) { if (p->cls == cls) n += weigh(p); p = p->next; }
          return n; }
        void sendall(const int *a, int n)
        { int i; for (i = 0; i LT n; i++) { while (!ready()) ; send(a[i]); } }
    (LT stands for the operator an XML comment cannot spell.)

    gcc gives visit and count two copies of `p = p->next; if (p) goto top;`,
    one after the call and one on the path that skips it; in count the copy
    after the call also holds the `n +=` gcc scheduled between the load and the
    test.  sendall's busy-wait and its outer loop share a header, but the
    busy-wait's latch is the header itself and the outer latch is different
    code, so nothing is joined.

    Pass 1 (default latch): visit (#1) and count (#2) are one `while (p)` loop
    with the `if` inside and the step after it; sendall keeps its inner loop
    (#3).  Pass 2 (option dedupitetail on, the old output): visit and count are
    a `do` loop whose first statement is a `while` loop (#4), sendall as before
    (#3).
-->
